---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="Hanfu Hairstyles: Traditional Styles by Dynasty"
  description="Hanfu hairstyles by dynasty: Tang high buns with floral ornaments, Song low coils, Ming crowns and buyao. Plus modern easy adaptations you can actually do."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.dresses-hanfu.com/guides/hairstyles/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Guides", url: "/guides" }, { name: "Hanfu Hairstyles", url: "/guides/hairstyles" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-08-08",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/guides/how-to-wear" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Hanfu Hairstyles</p>
          <h1>Hanfu Hairstyles: The Complete Guide</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            Hair is the finishing piece of any Hanfu look. Each dynasty had its signature silhouettes — and you can recreate most of them today with nothing more than pins, a comb, and a little practice.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>Hairstyles by Dynasty</h2>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Dynasty</th><th>Signature Style</th><th>Key Features</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Han</strong></td><td>Low, simple buns</td><td>Sleek and modest; a central parting with hair pulled into one low bun at the nape</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Tang</strong></td><td>High buns (daji)</td><td>Tall, full buns on top of the head, decorated with flowers and ornate hairpins</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Song</strong></td><td>Low coils, beizi-worn</td><td>Elegant low coils tucked at the ears, often with a beizi-style headband — restrained</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Ming</strong></td><td>Wash counters, crowns</td><td>Elaborate updos accented with the whistle: hanging ornaments (buyao) and the phoenix crown (fengguan)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">How to Do a Beginner Hanfu Updo</h2>
          <div class="step-list" style="max-width:100%;">
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">1</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Start with a Clean, Detangled Base</h3>
                <p>Brush hair thoroughly. For extra volume and hold, lightly tease the crown and apply a small amount of hairspray at the roots.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">2</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Create a High Ponytail</h3>
                <p>Gather hair at the crown and secure with a snug elastic. This is the foundation of nearly every Tang high-bun look — the higher the tail, the more dramatic the bun.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">3</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Form and Wrap the Bun</h3>
                <p>Twist the ponytail into a loop and wrap the tail around the base, securing as you go. For a classic daji, keep looping so the bun sits tall. Use crisscross pins (bobby pins) that enter at 45 degrees for real grip.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">4</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Add Hei (Hairpins) and Hair Accessories</h3>
                <p>Slide the zan perpendicular to the bun for structure, then add decorative flowers, buyao, or a headband. Match to your dynasty: flowers for the Tang, understated elegance for Song, gold/beads for Ming.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-row">
              <span class="step-number">5</span>
              <div class="step-content">
                <h3>Style the Front and Finish</h3>
                <p>Smooth, the hairline. For a softer look, leave a few wisps framing the face. Set with a light hold spray and add hair jewels or a silk ribbon for the final touch.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Easy Alternatives for Short or Fine Hair</h2>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Half-up styles</strong> — curl the lower half, pin the top in a small bun. Works for every dynasty.</li>
            <li><strong>Braided crowns</strong> — braid two sections at the front and pin across the crown like a halo.</li>
            <li><strong>Hair extension pieces</strong> — clip-on Chinese-style hair practice buns and coiled braids let you fake volume in seconds.</li>
            <li><strong>Wigs</strong> — many Hanfu photographers use full styled wigs for Tang high bun looks.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Accessorizing Hair</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The accessories complete the hairstyle: single-prong zan, two-prong chai, the hanging buyao ("step-shake"), floral ornaments, and the grand fengguan crown. Our <a href="/accessories/hairpins/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">complete hairpins guide</a> covers materials, prices, and how to pin them without damage.
          </p>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">Keep Learning</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/accessories/hairpins/" class="btn btn-outline">Hairpin Guide</a>
              <a href="/guides/etiquette/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Etiquette</a>
              <a href="/guides/how-to-wear/" class="btn btn-outline">How to Wear</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>